    "description": "•Lead Developer, Services team in zembly.com, a repository and development environment for Web services. Primarily responsible for identifying, designing and implementing services that are called internally (as well as externally) for running the zembly web application. Coordinated with other teams (primarily the UI team) to make sure that their back end requirements are well understood and designed. The team also implemented the gateway that allows services in zembly to be accessed outside of zembly  \n\n• Lead Developer, Web services/SOA team in Netbeans. This was a multi-site team whose members were located in California  and Prague, Czech Republic. Coordinated with various technical groups at Sun who were responsible for the runtimes (like JAX-WS implementation)  that were supported in Netbeans. Also cooperated and coordinated with the senior staff of the SeeBeyond team in southern California who relied on the team for many of its tool requirements. \nThe team developed facilities for creating, testing and deploying SOAP-based web services using JAX-WS, as well as RESTful web services using JAX-RS (JSR 311). The team also played a significant role in development of SOA tools (BPEL, XML Schema, WSDL editor) which were critical in the tooling support for the SOA development group . Kept track of developments in the web services arena, particularly the JSRs and other standards related to the technology.\n\n• Developer, Web services team in Java Studio Enterprise which provided facilities for creating and deploying J2EE (EE 5) web services. This included support for web applications, EJBs, and POJOs. Deliverables included code generators for  JSR 109 (Enterprise Web Services) artifacts that were deployable to Glassfish, WebLogic, Tomcat, and other application servers.",
